    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Approximately 1 in 5000 children in the United States exhibit symptoms of Marfan syndrome. A congenital aortic defect stemming from a mutation of the Fbn1 gene, Marfan syndrome typically presents with aortic root aneurysm caused by a combination of apoptosis, extracellular matrix (ECM) degradation, and oxidative stress. Numerous studies have attempted to address how oxidative stressors and reactive oxygen species (ROS) like superoxides can be reduced in the aortic root to attenuate aneurysm formation, although such studies remain inconclusive. The search for relatively non-toxic compounds that can successfully reduce oxidative stress in the aneurysm has yielded few results, although heme-oxygenase-1 (HO-1) appears to be a promising therapeutic target to reduce this stress. A common compound to induce HO-1 expression and activity is curcumin, an active ingredient in turmeric that can be administered orally in drinking water. To investigate this connection, both mice and cultured mouse smooth muscle cells (SMCs) were treated with curcumin to induce HO-1 expression; echocardiography, protein quantification, gene expression, and histology experiments were performed on the mice, while protein quantification and gene expression experiments were performed on the SMCs. The data presented here provide novel insight into the efficacy and viability of using HO-1 inducers to reduce oxidative stress on the aortic root and to attenuate formation of aortic root aneurysm in Marfan syndrome.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
